#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069578} :activity/id #uuid "09fac72c-e8cf-5881-9f87-6135d15c419b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048652 :topic/id #uuid "b27952f8-4bda-51a9-b51b-57fe61de4a72" :topic/math-academy-id 470} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069578 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070022 :tutorial/content "A **function** is a rule that takes a number as **input** and then **outputs** another number.\n\nFor instance, let's look at the following function: \n\n$$\nf(x) = 3x+1\n$$\n\nIn this example,\n\n- $f$ is the **name** of the function,\n\n- the number $x$ is the **input**,\n\n- the number $3x+1$ is the **output**.\n\nIf we **substitute** a number into our function in place of $x$, we get another number as output. \n\nFor example, by substituting $x={\\color{blue}{2}},$ we get\n\n$$\nf({\\color{blue}{2}}) = 3\\cdot {\\color{blue}{2}}+1 = 7.\n$$\n\nSo, the input $x={\\color{blue}{2}}$ results in an output of $7.$\n\nWe can substitute any number we like into this function! For example, if we substitute $x={\\color{red}{-2}},$ we get\n\n$$\nf({\\color{red}{-2}}) = 3\\cdot ({\\color{red}{-2}})+1 = -5.\n$$\n\nSo, the input $x={\\color{red}{-2}}$ results in an output of $-5.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "902a3802-7423-5fa0-9cf1-43856055c65c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8783 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "726a2782-726c-5f7e-b8e5-138b8563ebff" :step/math-academy-id 138156 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069579}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069579 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068701 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068486 :question/id #uuid "38228952-21ef-5caf-9805-76e42ebf0caa" :question/math-academy-id "e-14148"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e5366dd-a374-5abd-bdf6-77f378f97145" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048594} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049299} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050127}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069041} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069115} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069123} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233385} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233396} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233405} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233561} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233568} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233575} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482561} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482574} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492831} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492850} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492901} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522723}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Function"} :step/id #uuid "8af30817-9be7-537a-9bfb-2ce31b525422" :step/math-academy-id 138157 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069580}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069580 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070023 :tutorial/content "Functions with more than one input are called **multivariable functions**. \n\nFor example, the multivariable function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x-y\n$$\n\ntakes in two numbers, $x$ and $y$, and returns their difference. \n\nFor multivariable functions, the order of the inputs matters. For example, if we substitute $x={\\color{blue}{3}}$ and $y={\\color{red}{2}},$ we get\n\n$$\nf({\\color{blue}{3}},{\\color{red}{2}}) = {\\color{blue}{3}}-{\\color{red}{2}}=1,\n$$\n\nwhereas if we substitute $x={\\color{red}{2}}$ and $y={\\color{blue}{3}},$ we get a different output:\n\n$$\nf({\\color{red}{2}},{\\color{blue}{3}})={\\color{red}{2}}-{\\color{blue}{3}}=-1\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0bef6d6f-bda2-56dd-badb-0001ca2e4329" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8784 :tutorial/title "Evaluating Functions of More Than One Variable"} :step/id #uuid "004a3461-1ea4-5e28-807b-fea6c51ae3d4" :step/math-academy-id 138161 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069581}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069581 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068702 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068487 :question/id #uuid "4e06fa0b-f3f0-5e60-9a83-67a393b4cc07" :question/math-academy-id "e-14150"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2b5f42de-fad6-54b6-90e1-205fdbe8a456" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069042} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069116} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069124} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482526} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482569} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492852} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544826} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544830} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544841}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Multivariable Function"} :step/id #uuid "e7679093-39bc-5822-a013-8ae88e7f542e" :step/math-academy-id 138162}] :activity/title "Introduction to Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068995}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046648} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492946} :activity/id #uuid "32d73cc0-19b4-59d2-9583-c970a787e5ed" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048652 :topic/id #uuid "b27952f8-4bda-51a9-b51b-57fe61de4a72" :topic/math-academy-id 470} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492936 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492831 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e1864d1-334e-5ed1-a854-e574404fd88a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492368}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492368} :answer-field/id #uuid "40e76a0e-856c-5852-affb-986c061df288" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492730}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "135b40fb-5cf8-57bf-b723-676d052a026d" :question/math-academy-id "q-214832" :question/problem "If $f(x)={x}^{2}-{4}^{x-2},$ then $f(4)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We substitute $x=4$ into the function definition and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{2}-{4}^{x-2} \\\\ f(4) & ={4}^{2}-{4}^{4-2} \\\\  & =16-{4}^{2} \\\\  & =16-16 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "235c73d5-ae23-5b39-841a-4951560a5eff"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492946 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492850 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "019dfabe-776e-5411-a52a-468bd8010e2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "g(-3)=-29" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492226} {:answer/id #uuid "1d371280-0e81-5a2f-8015-37b237435f45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "g(-3)=21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492274} {:answer/id #uuid "28841ece-193a-5984-aca8-980d7d142fd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "g(-3)=29" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492301} {:answer/id #uuid "8bf18704-fb98-57f4-be11-81048d8cd298" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "g(-3)=49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492495} {:answer/id #uuid "8d6261f9-d215-5ae3-bfdb-9431ed756997" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "g(-3)=-21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492496}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492496} :answer-field/id #uuid "76bd600f-4df3-5837-8db3-4c610cb01b2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492754}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3e0ff0f4-e55f-55b3-97b5-30132033682d" :question/math-academy-id "q-12993" :question/problem "Evaluate $g(-3)$, where $g(x)=(x+1)^{2}-(x-2)^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We substitute $x=-3$ into the function definition and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & =(x+1)^{2}-(x-2)^{2} \\\\ g(-3) & =(-3+1)^{2}-(-3-2)^{2} \\\\  & =(-2)^{2}-(-5)^{2} \\\\  & =4-25 \\\\  & =-21\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "45066582-f649-5b97-b9fa-5d0d0116ad1b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492959}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492957 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492852 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ea9fe05-136c-54fa-8287-468235cbdb0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492316} {:answer/id #uuid "92ff2ec6-c1ed-57da-a105-85732944baad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492504} {:answer/id #uuid "c4dd854f-76b8-57b7-925b-6bec0085d762" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492611} {:answer/id #uuid "c81618e6-76ad-5708-8e7c-de8c549d1cea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492616} {:answer/id #uuid "cfb62b1a-34c6-56d6-983c-37f403386609" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492629}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492616} :answer-field/id #uuid "021d4422-18e9-573a-aea3-c035cc557e6f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492706}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3e8b84ea-82b6-5138-bf4d-e73af61fdac3" :question/math-academy-id "q-130576" :question/problem "Evaluate $f(-1,2)$ if $f(x,y)=(2x-y)(x+2y).$" :question/worked-solution "We substitute $x=-1$ and $y=2$ into the function definition and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =(2x-y)(x+2y) \\\\ f(-1,2) & =(2(-1)-2)((-1)+2(2)) \\\\  & =(-4)(3) \\\\  & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "719d3968-3672-57cb-89f5-452d75b3654a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492936}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492959 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492901 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04609b43-5111-5208-b18d-1c6e7b99af15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "g(-1)=-32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492230} {:answer/id #uuid "16f29b86-ae62-561b-a969-655e99943a2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "g(-1)=27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492260} {:answer/id #uuid "28262c89-1983-5b3b-9aa2-400cb80daebc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "g(-1)=31" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492299} {:answer/id #uuid "8319ede1-808f-592a-a403-f22857a5c9d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "g(-1)=-27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492481} {:answer/id #uuid "b5da9e45-5970-5fa3-92b6-9e353c5519c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "g(-1)=-39" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492584}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492230} :answer-field/id #uuid "7da22270-2bc9-5e75-a1ee-38a321ba33ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492758}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b9348d7e-52bc-5a07-98ca-88ef4ff5b0f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-31727" :question/problem "Evaluate $g(-1),$ if $g(x)=(x+3)^{2}-(x-5)^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We substitute $x=-1$ into the function definition and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & =(x+3)^{2}-(x-5)^{2} \\\\ g(-1) & =((-1)+3)^{2}-((-1)-5)^{2} \\\\  & =(2)^{2}-(-6)^{2} \\\\  & =4-36 \\\\  & =-32\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "75570071-e9a9-567a-93e7-3ab7178838f5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492957}}] :activity/title "Introduction to Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492206}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544957} :activity/id #uuid "f68627be-ff54-526c-8208-8406616b4322" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048652 :topic/id #uuid "b27952f8-4bda-51a9-b51b-57fe61de4a72" :topic/math-academy-id 470} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544957 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233385 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f66ec8e-8bae-5a96-a179-d8e3a1dfe2cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544118} {:answer/id #uuid "3182f7a3-fd37-55f9-853d-66f2f6f3f9d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544197} {:answer/id #uuid "514f3bb9-096e-5c0d-b085-0e9b207d4026" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544264} {:answer/id #uuid "5da83ddd-756d-5caa-827b-cb339578aa6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544290} {:answer/id #uuid "76c83aeb-cd5d-5dbb-bf1d-595dc42cd722" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544350}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544118} :answer-field/id #uuid "a932ff36-ed99-5a50-893b-5f175f3ceeef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544730}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "06658175-2088-56d6-9b3b-8c8339fcc59d" :question/math-academy-id "q-130569" :question/problem "Evaluate $f(2)$, where $f(x)=\\sqrt{x+7}+{4}^{x}.$" :question/worked-solution "We substitute $x=2$ into the function definition and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\sqrt{x+7}+{4}^{x} \\\\ f(2) & =\\sqrt{2+7}+{4}^{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9}+16 \\\\  & =3+16 \\\\  & =19\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a3365b0a-a746-531d-b973-83372efaf126" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544989}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544989 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544841 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "351d8210-c575-5ac4-a472-cd52a62017be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544204} {:answer/id #uuid "3c1e55c9-211f-5df0-b5a6-dab892b30f5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544218} {:answer/id #uuid "7736b0cd-e345-58aa-b572-63adfa1c527d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544352} {:answer/id #uuid "92061fea-8540-57d3-bf20-97d7a67ca306" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544406} {:answer/id #uuid "da61b983-1fd7-5312-8583-91abd7c70e0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544557}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544204} :answer-field/id #uuid "de4038dd-4a4f-5744-b3e0-9f127c2fa707" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544760}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "850f2473-aeb8-5637-89c4-7aa5503f51fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-130574" :question/problem "Evaluate $f(2,1)$ if $f(x,y)=(x-3y)(x+y)^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We substitute $x=2$ and $y=1$ into the function definition and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =(x-3y)(x+y)^{2} \\\\ f(2,1) & =(2-3(1))(2+1)^{2} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (3)^{2} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f310f4e8-5364-5f72-bc12-2c5b4ed0e307" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544995}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544995 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544830 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2522a912-6ec9-5d1e-9006-2d6a985f8f29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544168} {:answer/id #uuid "32bfe30f-860e-5462-9664-fd946b7e9aeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544200} {:answer/id #uuid "432f6ee1-afa8-57d6-ae54-4a08db2f0814" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544235} {:answer/id #uuid "92fc0bde-21ea-528f-87e4-e626ffa89252" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544411} {:answer/id #uuid "f5c66c90-2e89-5fdf-92e0-e36e92e33fac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544622}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544622} :answer-field/id #uuid "92a31c45-7d31-5b52-9c18-6a21a65bc008" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544720}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6e8aaddd-953c-576c-ad6b-2e4d36b66988" :question/math-academy-id "q-130578" :question/problem "Evaluate $f(1,1)$ if $f(x,y)=(3y-x)^{3}+(2x-y)^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We substitute $x=1$ and $y=1$ into the function definition and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =(3y-x)^{3}+(2x-y)^{2} \\\\ f(1,1) & =(3(1)-1)^{3}+(2(1)-1)^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{3}+{1}^{2} \\\\  & =8+1 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "fffd7ed9-55d6-58a4-b296-ec80636460e6"}] :activity/title "Introduction to Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544085}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074657} :activity/id #uuid "7809af08-83ac-5450-986d-2614a3fd22d3" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048826 :topic/id #uuid "c375d3cc-e398-5a47-a69f-ddc853431752" :topic/math-academy-id 487} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074657 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075033 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to solve, *without* using graphs, the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y &=4 \\\\ x-2 &=y\\,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn order to solve the system without a graph, we will need to combine the equations into a single equation that can be solved using algebraic manipulation.\n\nOne way to combine the equations is called the **substitution method.** To use the substitution method, we substitute for one variable in terms of the other.\n\nFor instance, notice that we can make $y$ the subject of the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\color{red}y=x - 2\n$$\n\nSubstituting this expression for $y$ into the first equation, we get an equation that we can solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y & =4 \\\\ x+(x-2) & =4 \\\\ 2x-2 & =4 \\\\ 2x & =6 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x=3.$ But we also need to figure out what $y$ is. We can do this by substituting $x=3$ back into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x-2 \\\\ y & =3-2 \\\\ y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x = 3$ and $y = 1$, which can be written as $\\left(3, 1\\right).$\n\n**Note:** Whenever we isolate the variable in one equation, we have to substitute it into the *other* equation. We cannot substitute a variable into the same equation it came from, because this will not give us any more information about the variable." :tutorial/id #uuid "e1e8cb2d-3d4e-5cb9-8947-2bd9deb11b89" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8131 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "413b9b90-7693-5568-a868-970271a5a258" :step/math-academy-id 197419 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074658}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074658 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073752 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073445 :question/id #uuid "e9aae52c-65f3-5dff-bc6b-a530f8c7acc1" :question/math-academy-id "e-13009"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e79a91b-c479-53da-9f06-59c082fa4187" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048678} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050622}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508241} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074030} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074172} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074185} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233560} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233567} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233574} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496226} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496255} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497411} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503753} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508171} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508174} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508179} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508187} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508204} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508205} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508207} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508210} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508214} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508215} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508227} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508233} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546971} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558573} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562396}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Linear Equations Using Substitution"} :step/id #uuid "99e9f36f-648c-5025-bfca-c63f6f77d9b1" :step/math-academy-id 197420 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074659}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074659 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073753 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073446 :question/id #uuid "707504e8-a367-5dc2-a663-371a9dcf7224" :question/math-academy-id "e-13010"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4352698-7af7-5d74-9c3a-0f951c3e6078" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505092} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073997} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073998} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074047} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496252} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496314} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505001} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505002} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505004} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505005} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505044} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505050} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505053} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505062} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505067} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505080} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505081} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505084} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546991}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Challenging System of Linear Equations Using Substitution"} :step/id #uuid "30b3f613-45c5-50c5-bd79-5c12149aea27" :step/math-academy-id 197424}] :activity/title "Solving Systems of Equations by Substitution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073923}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547088} :activity/id #uuid "7311ef08-ecf2-579b-9876-4973ab9af82b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048826 :topic/id #uuid "c375d3cc-e398-5a47-a69f-ddc853431752" :topic/math-academy-id 487} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547088 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074185 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "831b8a12-3240-5543-8f0b-63a066174430" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186071349} {:answer/id #uuid "e71abec1-af90-564e-96cc-65f749dff99a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186071350} {:answer/id #uuid "f9bd14c8-f201-5c70-ba77-5489006197a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186071351} {:answer/id #uuid "30891c0a-e32e-5e99-9826-2629f5f5792b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186071352} {:answer/id #uuid "424e6c76-ef65-55f4-bb2c-05129500785f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186071353}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186071349} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ff0f62d-b46f-5fed-abea-e9c339cbff76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186071348}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5c71bd6b-b9a3-5587-89f4-a14a2c760bc2" :question/math-academy-id "q-5179" :question/problem "Given the system of equations below, what is the value of $p-q?$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3p=15 \\\\ p+2q=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. It doesn't matter which equation we pick first, so we choose the easiest one. In this case, the first equation looks like the easiest.\n\nUsing the first equation, we can isolate $p$ by dividing both sides of the equation by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3p & =15 \\\\ p & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $p=5$ into $p+2q=9,$ then we can find the value of $q:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p+2q & =9 \\\\ (5)+2q & =9 \\\\ 2q & =4 \\\\ q & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $p=5,\\,q=2.$ So,\n\n$$\np-q=5-2=3.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0a9225c0-5f39-5569-928a-3834451e9d52" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547152}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547152 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546991 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b85cb2c-460d-5f91-86c0-674d13fae83e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546309} {:answer/id #uuid "242eed59-c937-5dab-b478-b3a1accb9d4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546329} {:answer/id #uuid "463e0a8b-5e11-54a2-8cdd-72b176626176" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546407} {:answer/id #uuid "6a785a85-c477-5db9-a10d-570f963f9181" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546485} {:answer/id #uuid "f5ff80ce-ffb0-542a-ba50-ce8557612884" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546805}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546309} :answer-field/id #uuid "e282d81b-1bfe-5c5b-abe6-d91779057814" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546940}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3b789afd-79c7-56fc-b94a-f894f177dfe2" :question/math-academy-id "q-18555" :question/problem "Given the system of equations below, what is the value of $a+b?$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2b=6a+14 \\\\ 5b+25=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We number the equations so that we can refer to them more easily:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2b=6a+14 & \\,\\,(1) \\\\ 5b+25=0. & \\,\\,(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. Using equation $(2),$ we can solve for $b$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5b+25 & =0 \\\\ 5b & =-25 \\\\ b & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $b=-5$ into equation $(1)$ and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-5) & =6a+14 \\\\ -10 & =6a+14 \\\\ -10-14 & =6a \\\\ 6a & =-24 \\\\ a & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $a=-4,\\,b=-5.$ So,\n\n$$\na+b=-4+(-5)=-4-5=-9.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ca7de6ad-e459-52ba-851d-06dd86cc6059" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547164}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547164 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546971 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25d7a53c-5916-5db7-992b-ff7a626a51c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546336} {:answer/id #uuid "51399b6f-2763-5014-9b02-635633f0dada" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546427} {:answer/id #uuid "9e23c320-e17b-534f-8a91-354568ebc163" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546603} {:answer/id #uuid "a3f272eb-ffc1-5fb6-b622-055a0def12d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546617} {:answer/id #uuid "d9f37f2b-79d8-5ebd-8615-8e1bcfb28380" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546732}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546732} :answer-field/id #uuid "78ca5c4c-b502-5598-a6f0-d81d606b319e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546881}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1ad70587-af92-55e0-95cf-31aa82c10e13" :question/math-academy-id "q-52115" :question/problem "Given the system of equations below, what is the value of $a-b?$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}a=7 \\\\ 3a+6b=15\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. It so happens that the first equation is already solved for $a:$\n\n$$\na=7\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $a=7$ into $3a+6b=15,$ then we can find the value of $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a+6b & =15 \\\\ 3(7)+6b & =15 \\\\ 6b & =-6 \\\\ b & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $a=7,\\,b=-1.$ So,\n\n$$\na-b=7-(-1)=7+1=8.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e5c9a09f-e906-5432-9fef-4ec9ba80344e"}] :activity/title "Solving Systems of Equations by Substitution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546244}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055608} :activity/id #uuid "581ada9e-b8f9-5513-9a35-8ae3af36139e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049074 :topic/id #uuid "d85c70bd-27ec-54bb-a459-275d176f6b8b" :topic/math-academy-id 89} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055608 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056069 :tutorial/content "Let's think about how to factor the following trinomial:\n\n$$\nx^2 + 7x + 12\n$$\n\nThis trinomial is neither a perfect square nor a difference of squares. In such cases, the factoring process requires a little trial and error.\n\nWe can factor this trinomial in four steps:\n\n- **Step 1:** Find the factor pairs of the constant term $(12)$:The factor pairs of $12$ are\n$(1, 12), (2, 6), (3, 4)$.\n- **Step 2:** Determine which of these factor pairs add up to the coefficient of the linear term $(7)$:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 + 12|= 13 & \\cdot \\\\ 2 + 6|= 8 & \\cdot \\\\ 3 + 4|= 7 & ✓\\end{bmatrix}$\n- **Step 3:** Using the factor pair identified in the previous step, write the linear term as $7x=3x+4x$:\n$x^{2} + 7x + 12|=; x^{2} + 3x + 4x_(⏟)_(7x) + 12$\n- **Step 4:** Factor the first two terms and the last two terms separately and then factor the expression completely:\n$x^{2} + 3x + 4x + 12|=; (x^{2} + 3x) + (4x + 12)|=; x(x + 3) + 4(x + 3)|=; (x + 4)(x + 3)$\n\nAnd we're done!\n\nBefore we continue, note the following:\n\n- The process carried out in step 4 is sometimes called **factoring by grouping.** This is because we group two sets of terms and factor them individually before factoring the entire expression.\n- We can check that our answer is correct by multiplying out the factors:\n$(x + 4)(x + 3)|= x(x + 3) + 4(x + 3); = x^{2} + 3x + 4x + 12; = x^{2} + 7x + 12✓$" :tutorial/id #uuid "90a8aa34-4549-57c2-bdc2-24cc962cd306" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8891 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "add682be-a6dd-51b0-a261-ba8a3f84b958" :step/math-academy-id 178072 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055609}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055609 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054658 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054476 :question/id #uuid "1e0116a2-6f90-5b57-97c1-6a64b45c9cef" :question/math-academy-id "e-14343"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c9ba00d-9171-5732-8539-2e2943cce58e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048351} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051017}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055108} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055495} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055497} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233563} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233570} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233577}] :knowledge-point/title "Factoring Trinomials With Positive Coefficients"} :step/id #uuid "072ef290-5a3e-575d-a0fd-7d8084343f23" :step/math-academy-id 178073 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055610}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055610 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056070 :tutorial/content "Let's now consider the following trinomial:\n\n$$\nx^2 - 2x - 8\n$$\n\nNotice that the constant term $(-8)$ is *negative*. So, when writing down our factor pairs, one number will always be positive, and the other will always be negative.\n\nSo, first, we write down all factor pairs of $-8{:}$\n\n$$\n(1,-8), (-1, 8), (2,-4), (-2, 4)\n$$\n\nNow, we find which factor pair adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(-2)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 + (-8) &= -7 & \\cdot \\\\ (-1) + 8|= 7 & \\cdot \\\\ 2 + (-4)|=-2 & ✓ \\\\ (-2) + 4|= 2 & \\cdot\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the factor pair identified above, write the linear term as\n\n$$\n-2x = 2x-4x\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 2x - 8 &=  \\\\\n&= x^{2} + 2x - 4x - 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the first two terms and the last two terms separately, and then factor the expression completely:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + 2x - 4x - 8 &=  \\\\\n(x^{2} + 2x) + (-4x - 8) &=  \\\\\nx(x + 2) - 4(x + 2) &=  \\\\\n&= (x - 4)(x + 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's see another example." :tutorial/id #uuid "3b5a5752-232b-5714-b74a-1e50c4210b83" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8894 :tutorial/title "Trinomials With Negative Constants"} :step/id #uuid "232a2780-b6bf-56f2-b607-6b8d27ae3f25" :step/math-academy-id 178077 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055611}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055611 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054659 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054478 :question/id #uuid "41c3e610-0259-59db-9494-3af6f47c7360" :question/math-academy-id "e-14350"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ac258511-5028-5fc8-9843-99309b36a38e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055130} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055231} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459240} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480700} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487383} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498516}] :knowledge-point/title "Factoring Trinomials With Negative Constants"} :step/id #uuid "47e26748-840d-518f-8679-595bc9b0406d" :step/math-academy-id 178078 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055612}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055612 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056076 :tutorial/content "Remember that to factor a quadratic expression, we must find a factor pair of the constant term that adds to the coefficient of the linear term.\n\nWith that in mind, let's factor the following trinomial:\n\n$$\nx^2 - 7x+12\n$$\n\nNotice that,\n\n- the constant term $(12)$ is *positive*, while\n- the coefficient of the linear term $(-7)$ is *negative*.\n\nSince the constant is *positive* and the coefficient of the linear term is *negative*, we must first consider the *negative* factor pairs of the constant term $(12)$. These are as follows:\n\n$$\n(-1,-12),\\qquad (-2,-6),\\qquad (-3,-4)\n$$\n\nFor each pair, their *product* is positive, and their *sum* is negative, which is what we want.\n\nNow, we find which factor pair adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(-7)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}(-1) + (-12) &= -13 & \\cdot \\\\ (-2) + (-6)|=-8 & \\cdot \\\\ (-3) + (-4)|=-7 & ✓\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the factor pair identified in the previous step, write the linear term as\n\n$$\n-7x = -3x - 4x\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 7x + 12 &=  \\\\\n&= x^{2} - 3x - 4x + 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor the first two terms and the last two terms separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 3x - 4x + 12 &=  \\\\\n(x^{2} - 3x) + (-4x + 12) &=  \\\\\n&= x(x - 3) + 4(-x + 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to get a *common* factor in both terms, we take an additional factor of $-1$ from the second term:\n\n$$\nx(x - 3) - 4(x - 3)\n$$\n\nNow that we have a common factor of $(x-3)$, we factor the expression fully in the usual way:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(x - 3) - 4(x - 3) &=  \\\\\n&= (x - 3)(x - 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "25f481d5-38d8-5f35-95fc-dd74f97d0394" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9504 :tutorial/title "Trinomials With Negative Linear Coefficients and Positive Constants"} :step/id #uuid "47589875-8a7f-5cea-a2cf-0da701bb35ac" :step/math-academy-id 178082 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055613}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055613 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054660 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054477 :question/id #uuid "f51ebe90-e58a-5712-84b8-4367ed18d58e" :question/math-academy-id "e-14346"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65a95770-9419-5a8f-b264-58cca3e3c0db" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055109} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055131} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055496}] :knowledge-point/title "Factoring Trinomials With Negative Linear Coefficients and Positive Constants"} :step/id #uuid "87945626-63e1-5990-b1f8-6d13138cf85c" :step/math-academy-id 178083}] :activity/title "Factoring Trinomials" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054931}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134608} :activity/id #uuid "25739194-82c1-5788-9ee1-b6fee61cbd79" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049399 :topic/id #uuid "f1aa8ae2-23cf-5d91-8af0-a43e9db5e668" :topic/math-academy-id 2214} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134608 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135191 :tutorial/content "The explicit formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence is\n\n$$\na_n = a_1 + (n-1)d, \\quad n \\geq 1.\n$$\n\nEvery arithmetic sequence can also be written using the following recursive formula:\n\n![](images/a2/a2d28772cb4cb6078d2fccdca054ec1c2dccf27a5ea0b5972b9ac174eaad671f.svg)\n\nFor example, suppose that we're given the following explicit formula for an arithmetic sequence:\n\n$$\na_n =2n+6, \\quad n\\geq 1\n$$\n\nTo convert to a recursive formula, we just need to work out the first term and common difference. The first two terms are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =2(1)+6=8 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =2(2)+6=10,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the common difference is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =10-8 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first term is $a_1={\\color{blue}8}$ and the common difference is $d={\\color{red}2}.$ Therefore, the recursive formula is as follows:\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = a_n + {\\color{red}2}, \\quad a_1 = {\\color{blue}8}, \\quad n \\geq 1\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b6cd14c7-922b-5e05-9c8e-b842717155ad" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8910 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "02ae2395-79da-5dc3-9f17-a75936abdc07" :step/math-academy-id 191930 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134609}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134609 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133746 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133531 :question/id #uuid "00113d76-b950-513e-a3ea-29eef91c9cec" :question/math-academy-id "e-14383"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b8b10a8-f81a-5dae-b54e-b10959e3ad14" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050157} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050380}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134163} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134446} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560987} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561011}] :knowledge-point/title "Converting an Explicit Formula to a Recursive Formula"} :step/id #uuid "ed82570d-8cc5-564d-8da6-d86c3395fced" :step/math-academy-id 191931 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134610}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134610 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133747 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133532 :question/id #uuid "e04ad3e0-888e-51c0-b60c-e816b7df514b" :question/math-academy-id "e-14386"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d36a3a61-e23b-5ce3-ac65-f7ac1691ec4e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134177} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134447} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560975} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561005}] :knowledge-point/title "Converting an Explicit Formula to a Recursive Formula Using Function Notation"} :step/id #uuid "340d50dc-babe-5074-be5e-52e39f437e88" :step/math-academy-id 191934 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134611}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134611 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135192 :tutorial/content "It's often more useful to translate from the recursive formula to an explicit one. So how do we find the explicit formula for the arithmetic sequence defined recursively as\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = a_n + {\\color{red}1},\\, \\quad a_1 = {\\color{blue}6},\\, \\quad n\\geq 1?\n$$\n\nThe explicit formula is\n\n$$\na_n = a_1 + (n-1)d, \\quad n \\geq 1,\n$$\n\nso we just need to determine the first term $a_1$ and the common difference $d.$\n\nWe are given that the first term is $a_1={\\color{blue}6},$ and we can use the recursive rule to compute the second term, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & ={a}_{1}+1 \\\\  & =6+1 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the first and second terms, we can compute the common difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =7-6 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $a_1 = {\\color{blue}6}$ and $d={\\color{red}1}$ into the formula for the $n$th term:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\na_n&= a_{1}+(n-1)d \\[3pt]\n&= {\\color{blue}6} + (n - 1)({\\color{red}1}) \\[3pt]\n& =6 + n-1\\[3pt]\n&=n+5\n}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\na_n=n+5, \\quad n \\geq 1.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c6c5b9f8-4c71-5c1f-8841-3f22646e5074" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8914 :tutorial/title "Translating From the Recursive to the Explicit Formula"} :step/id #uuid "7cd53e1d-4c0c-570b-bef4-68f8081c2fef" :step/math-academy-id 191938 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134612}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134612 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133748 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133533 :question/id #uuid "d732cfb5-9db5-535d-adb1-5d3292f1c497" :question/math-academy-id "e-14389"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36e1e439-ec4a-5577-80d5-3b113bf36e74" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134056} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134164} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233564} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233571} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233578} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560995} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561033}] :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Recursive Formula to an Explicit Formula"} :step/id #uuid "2a238f59-a780-5248-bfa5-a217bec91d6c" :step/math-academy-id 191939 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134613}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134613 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133749 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133534 :question/id #uuid "26b3b89d-d485-51ac-b964-8377ce3aff28" :question/math-academy-id "e-14393"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "baac268a-629e-5887-885a-49393c72776f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134178} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134479} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560974} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561053}] :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Recursive Formula to an Explicit Formula Using Function Notation"} :step/id #uuid "c81f0b63-4b36-56b0-bc0f-1535d35453ef" :step/math-academy-id 191943}] :activity/title "Translating Between Explicit and Recursive Formulas for Arithmetic Sequences" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134049}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079457} :activity/id #uuid "45c505a4-630e-59ff-9596-9a36627fb33e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050396 :topic/id #uuid "457136ef-67f9-50bb-b078-34de9c6b59a9" :topic/math-academy-id 655} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079457 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079621 :tutorial/content "A **polynomial term** is a term where the variable contains a non-negative integer power. (The non-negative integers consist of the numbers $0$, $1$, $2$, $3$, and so on.)\n\nSome examples of polynomial terms include\n\n$$\nx^2, \\qquad \\dfrac 1 3 x^6,\\qquad x, \\qquad 1\n$$\n\nLet's go through each one:\n\n- $x^{\\color{blue}2}$ is a polynomial term because the power of the variable is ${\\color{blue}2}$, a non-negative integer.\n- $\\dfrac{1}{3} x^{\\color{blue}6}$ is a polynomial term because the power of the variable is ${\\color{blue}6}$, which is also a non-negative integer. The coefficient of $\\dfrac 1 3$ isn't important.\n- $x$ is a polynomial term because it can be thought of as $x^{\\color{blue}1}$. So the power of the variable is ${\\color{blue}1}$, which is a non-negative integer.\n- Finally, $1$ is a polynomial term because it can be expressed as $1\\cdot x^{\\color{blue}0}$. So the power of the variable is ${\\color{blue}0}$, which is a non-negative integer.\n\nOn the other hand, the following expressions are *not* polynomial terms because their powers are not non-negative integers:\n\n$$\n4x^{-2}, \\qquad x^{1/3}, \\qquad {\\sqrt{\\color{black}x}}, \\qquad {\\dfrac{\\color{black}1}{x^3}}\n$$\n\nTo see why, let's go through each one:\n\n- $4x^{\\color{red}-2}$ is not a polynomial term because the power of the variable is ${\\color{red}-2}$, which is negative.\n- $x^{\\color{red}1/3}$ is not a polynomial term because the power of the variable is ${\\color{red}1/3}$, which is not an integer.\n- $\\sqrt x$ is not a polynomial term because it can be written as $x^{\\color{red}1/2}$. So the power of the variable is ${\\color{red}1/2}$, which is not an integer.\n- Finally, $\\dfrac 1 {x^3}$ is not a polynomial term because it can be written as $x^{-\\color{red}3}$. So the power of the variable is ${\\color{red}-3}$, which is negative." :tutorial/id #uuid "a11e9fb3-6fe1-547c-996f-fb83d15a47d0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1578 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0500aa37-469a-53bd-a5da-8fbb0fe569cc" :step/math-academy-id 4106 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079458}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079458 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078529 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078153 :question/id #uuid "0690af9e-ae28-50a0-b2fd-0a9c3baaec3e" :question/math-academy-id "e-1280"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c12f648d-f276-5123-b6aa-b222ad108825" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049089} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050495}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079068} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079149}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Polynomial Terms"} :step/id #uuid "ad32752d-72d3-5211-adbf-f50f0a51075b" :step/math-academy-id 4107 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079459}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079459 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079622 :tutorial/content "A **polynomial** is a sum of polynomial terms, such as\n$x^2 -5x +3, \\qquad x^3 + x^2 - 9x, \\qquad 4x^4 -2x^2, \\qquad 3x^6 - 2x^3+x^2-1$." :tutorial/id #uuid "2b356365-33dd-5603-8027-95f09e3184a7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1579 :tutorial/title "Polynomials"} :step/id #uuid "a43ff34d-c027-5df2-820c-70b022c83dc9" :step/math-academy-id 4109 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079460}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079460 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078530 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078155 :question/id #uuid "f6c0be28-9441-513e-83c5-866292fd307a" :question/math-academy-id "e-1282"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "df8ab577-24c5-5ac4-a9de-9ecc7dfb6653" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078922} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079069}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Polynomials"} :step/id #uuid "2556eb4e-afab-5ae3-8996-7aa3b8f721fc" :step/math-academy-id 4112 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079461}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079461 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079623 :tutorial/content "To **evaluate** a polynomial at a specific value of $x$, we substitute the value of $x$ into the polynomial and simplify.\n\nFor example, to evaluate the polynomial $2x^2+3$ at $x=5$, we substitute $x=5$ into the expression and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x^{2} + 3 &=  \\\\\n2(5)^{2} + 3 &=  \\\\\n2(25) + 3 &=  \\\\\n50 + 3 &=  \\\\\n&= 53\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d8de8488-40d3-593a-a8a1-9193eb94f8e8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1580 :tutorial/title "Evaluating Polynomials"} :step/id #uuid "64a56fd4-dcbc-5271-8a10-da5817638249" :step/math-academy-id 4114 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079462}} {:db/id #edb/ref 1759218